  "title": "Multi-channel EEG recordings during a sustained-attention driving task",
  "description": "This dataset provides BIDS-EEG formatted raw recordings from 27 healthy adults performing a sustained-attention driving task in a virtual-reality driving simulator on a motion platform. It comprises 62 sessions (~82 hours) of 32-channel EEG at 500 Hz plus a vehicle lateral-position channel. Events mark randomly induced lane-departure onsets and the participants' steering responses, allowing study of reaction time as an index of fatigue and drowsiness.",
  "methods_description": "Participants drove a night-time monotonous highway at constant 100 km/h in a VR simulator on a six-degree-of-freedom Stewart motion platform, with random left/right lane-departure events and counter-steering responses. EEG was recorded with a Compumedics Neuroscan SynAmps2 Express amplifier and a 32-channel Quik-Cap (30 scalp electrodes plus A1/A2 mastoid references) at 500 Hz, 16-bit, with impedance below 5 kOhm. Vehicle position was recorded as an additional channel. Electrode positions are nominal 10-20 template coordinates.",
